# Issue #5909 — Scrub by Mouse Drag Mishandles Next Video

## ROOT CAUSE

`iina/PlayerCore.swift:952-964` (`seek(percent:forceExact:)`) — combined with `iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:606-633` (`updatePlayTime`) — the seek triggered on each drag tick is allowed to land at end-of-file (mpv then auto-advances to the next playlist item), and the very next mpv property update unconditionally rewrites `playSlider.doubleValue`, which causes `NSSlider`'s tracking loop to re-fire `playSliderChanges` for the *new* file from the same mouse position, repeating the cycle.

## CONTROL FLOW

1. User clicks the play slider thumb. `PlaySliderCell.startTracking` (`iina/PlaySliderCell.swift:175-183`) calls `playerCore.pause()` and returns `true` so `NSSliderCell` enters its tracking loop.
2. As the user drags, `NSSliderCell` continuously recomputes `doubleValue` from the mouse position and (because the slider is continuous) sends its action `playSliderChanges:` whenever the value changes.
3. `MainWindowController.playSliderChanges` (`iina/MainWindowController.swift:3246-3262`) gates on `state != .loading`, then forwards to the parent.
4. `PlayerWindowController.playSliderChanges` (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:689-693`) computes `percentage = 100 * sender.doubleValue / sender.maxValue` and calls `player.seek(percent: percentage, forceExact: !followGlobalSeekTypeWhenAdjustSlider)`.
5. When the user drags past the right edge of the slider, the cell saturates `doubleValue` at `maxValue=100` (`PlaySliderCell.awakeFromNib`, `iina/PlaySliderCell.swift:40-43`), so percentage is `100`.
6. In `PlayerCore.seek(percent:)` the conditional clamp `percent.clamped(to: 0..<100)` (line 959, half-open Range → returns `nextDown(100)` ≈ `99.999…`) only fires when `info.videoDuration?.second > 0`. The mpv `seek <pct> absolute-percent[+exact]` command is then issued.
7. As the in-line comment at lines 954-956 explicitly admits, this clamp "still won't work for videos with large keyframe interval" — and it also does nothing during the brief windows when `videoDuration` is `nil`/`0` (e.g. the moment between `MPV_EVENT_END_FILE` and the next `fileLoaded`). mpv treats the seek as EOF and, because `keep-open` is configured from `playlistAutoPlayNext`, automatically advances to the next playlist item (`iina/MPVController.swift:384-393`).
8. When the next file starts mpv emits `MPV_EVENT_START_FILE` (state → `.starting`), then `MPV_EVENT_FILE_LOADED` runs `PlayerCore.fileLoaded` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2086-2154`) which assigns the new `info.videoDuration`/`videoPosition`, and `MPV_EVENT_PLAYBACK_RESTART` (`iina/MPVController.swift:1169`) calls `playbackRestarted` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2302-2320`) which calls `syncUI(.time)`. That path lands in `PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime` (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:606-633`).
9. `updatePlayTime` at lines 628-631 unconditionally writes `playSlider.doubleValue = percentage` for the new file (≈ `0`). It does *not* check whether the slider is currently being tracked.
10. The cell's tracking loop is still running. On the next drag event it recomputes `doubleValue` from the cursor (still at the right edge) → back to `100`. Because the value just changed (`0 → 100`), `NSSliderCell` fires `playSliderChanges:` again. Step 4 repeats against the *new* file, causing another EOF seek, another auto-advance — and the cascade continues for as long as the user holds the mouse past the slider's right edge.

## WHY IT CASCADES

There are two compounding defects. First, the percent-based clamp in `PlayerCore.seek(percent:)` is not a sufficient EOF guard: it only fires when `info.videoDuration > 0`, the clamp ceiling (`nextDown(100.0)`) can still resolve to "past the last decodable frame" for files with large keyframe intervals (the comment at `PlayerCore.swift:954-956` documents this), and `forceExact` does not help when the precondition for clamping isn't satisfied — so the slider seek can and does land at EOF, and mpv (with `keep-open` set per `playlistAutoPlayNext`) auto-advances the playlist. Second, and more importantly for the cascade, `PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime` at line 630 rewrites `playSlider.doubleValue` from the file-loaded/playback-restart path even while the slider's `NSSliderCell` is mid-track. That external write to ~`0` followed by `NSSliderCell` recomputing the value to ~`100` from the still-pinned mouse position is what re-fires the action — turning what should have been *one* boundary seek into a fresh "seek to ~100%" against every newly loaded file. Each cycle takes ~one playback-restart round trip, so the user sees the player tear through several playlist entries as long as they keep the cursor past the slider's right edge.

## SUGGESTED FIX

Break the re-entry loop where it lives, in `PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime`: do not overwrite `playSlider.doubleValue` while the slider's cell is being tracked. The simplest one-liner is to gate the assignment on `!(playSlider.cell?.isHighlighted ?? false)` (NSSliderCell sets `isHighlighted` for the duration of `startTracking`/`stopTracking`), or equivalently maintain an `isPausedBeforeSeeking`-style flag set in `PlaySliderCell.startTracking`/`stopTracking` and consulted at `PlayerWindowController.swift:630`. With the slider's value pinned by the user during drag, the action will fire at most once per actual mouse movement and the cascade collapses to a single boundary event. As a defense-in-depth complement, tighten `PlayerCore.seek(percent:)` so it can never request EOF from a slider drag — drop the `duration > 0` precondition (always clamp), and prefer an absolute-seconds seek clamped to `duration - smallEpsilon` (e.g. one frame, or 0.1 s) so the keyframe-interval edge case noted in the existing comment no longer trips mpv into auto-advancing.
